Article 9 A loan contract between natural persons may be deemed to have been formed under any of the following circumstances:
(1) in the case of payment in cash, from the time the borrower receives the loan;
(2) in the case of payment by bank transfer, online electronic remittance, or other similar means, from the time the funds reach the borrower's account;
(3) in the case of delivery by negotiable instrument, from the time the borrower lawfully obtains the rights under the negotiable instrument;
(4) where the lender authorizes the borrower to control a specific fund account, from the time the borrower obtains actual control over such account; and
(5) where the lender provides the loan in another manner agreed with the borrower and actually performs the obligation.

Article 14 The People's Court shall find a private lending contract void under any of the following circumstances:
(1) obtaining a loan from a financial institution and re-lending it;
(2) re-lending funds obtained by borrowing from other for-profit legal persons, raising funds from its own employees, or illegally absorbing deposits from the public;
(3) a lender who has not lawfully obtained lending qualification provides loans to unspecified members of the public for profit-making purposes;
(4) the lender knew or should have known in advance that the borrower would use the loan for illegal or criminal activities but still provided the loan;
(5) violating the mandatory provisions of laws or administrative regulations; or
(6) violating public order and good customs.

Article 19 When hearing a private lending dispute case, if the People's Court finds any of the following circumstances, it shall strictly examine facts such as the cause, time, place, source of funds, delivery method, flow of funds, and the relationship and economic status of the lending parties, and comprehensively determine whether it constitutes a false civil action:
(1) the lender is clearly incapable of lending;
(2) the facts and reasons on which the lender bases its action are clearly inconsistent with common sense;
(3) the lender cannot submit debt instruments or the submitted debt instruments may be forged;
(4) both parties have participated in private lending actions multiple times within a certain period;
(5) a party refuses to appear in court without justifiable reasons, and the authorized agent's statements on the lending facts are unclear or contradictory;
(6) the parties have no dispute over the occurrence of the lending fact, or the claims and defenses are clearly inconsistent with common sense;
(7) the borrower's spouse or partner, or other creditors of a person not involved in the case raise factual objections;
(8) the parties have transferred property at a low price in other disputes;
(9) a party improperly renounces rights; or
(10) other circumstances where a false private lending action may exist.
